WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.046 --> 00:00:05.675
Young Ebenezer and Dick Wilkins didn't need to be told twice by old Fezziwig.

2
00:00:05.676 --> 00:00:11.739
Quick as a wink, the young men cleared away the desks and other furniture in the warehouse.

3
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:17.898
Soon the large room looked as open and inviting as a ballroom.

4
00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:22.841
In came a fiddler who began playing a joyous holiday tune.

5
00:00:22.842 --> 00:00:30.417
Mrs. Fezziwig arrived next, all smiles, followed by her daughters.

6
00:00:30.418 --> 00:00:35.485
There was dancing and music and laughter and good cheer.

7
00:00:35.486 --> 00:00:42.049
Several guests approached Fezziwig to thank him.

8
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:47.870
As he watched the scene before him, Scrooge seemed to become a different person.

9
00:00:47.871 --> 00:00:57.275
Unaware of what he was doing, he hummed along with the music, watched the dancers, and sniffed the food.

10
00:00:57.276 --> 00:01:00.780
He nearly forgot about the spirit standing beside him.

11
00:01:00.781 --> 00:01:07.353
But then the ghost pointed to the guests lined up to talk to Fezziwig.

12
00:01:07.354 --> 00:01:13.575
"It looks like your former boss didn't have to do much to earn the gratitude of these folks.

13
00:01:13.576 --> 00:01:19.026
He spent only a small amount of money to put on this Christmas party.

14
00:01:19.027 --> 00:01:25.454
Tell me, is that enough for him to deserve such praise and gratefulness from his guests?"

15
00:01:25.455 --> 00:01:31.976
"Yes!" Scrooge blurted out, suddenly remembering how much he had admired Fezziwig.

16
00:01:31.977 --> 00:01:37.527
"He was our boss, and he could make us happy or unhappy.

17
00:01:37.528 --> 00:01:41.100
It doesn't matter how much he spent on the party.

18
00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:48.128
The happiness he gave us was so great— it's as if it cost him a fortune. I . . ."

19
00:01:48.129 --> 00:01:53.475
Scrooge's words trailed off and he went silent.

20
00:01:53.476 --> 00:01:57.754
"What's the matter?" the ghost asked.

21
00:01:57.755 --> 00:02:01.570
"Nothing," Scrooge said, looking down.

22
00:02:01.571 --> 00:02:09.893
"I just wish I could say a word or two to my own employee right now, that's all."

23
00:02:09.894 --> 00:02:14.569
The ghost reached toward Scrooge again.

24
00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:20.090
"My time with you is growing short. We must move on now."

25
00:02:20.091 --> 00:02:27.395
After another touch from the ghost, Scrooge found himself in a shabby bedroom that he'd never seen before.

26
00:02:27.396 --> 00:02:31.068
A woman sat up in bed, looking tired.

27
00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:35.277
She was holding a tiny baby bundled in a blanket.

28
00:02:35.278 --> 00:02:40.552
"Where are we, spirit?" Scrooge asked, feeling confused.

29
00:02:40.553 --> 00:02:44.540
"I thought you were showing me things from my own past.

30
00:02:44.541 --> 00:02:50.569
I've never been to this place before, and I don't know that woman."

31
00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:55.762
The ghost pointed to the door just as it opened, and a man came in.

32
00:02:55.763 --> 00:03:02.950
It was Bob Cratchit, Scrooge's clerk, looking several years younger than he was.

33
00:03:02.951 --> 00:03:08.617
"Here's your water, my love," Cratchit said, handing a cup to the woman with the baby.

34
00:03:08.618 --> 00:03:15.558
"Thank you." She accepted it with a smile and had a long drink.

35
00:03:15.559 --> 00:03:19.755
"Would you like to hold our new son, Bob?"

36
00:03:19.756 --> 00:03:25.382
"Do you think it's all right?" Cratchit looked eager but also nervous.

37
00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:31.994
"He's so much smaller than our others were when they were born! I don't want to hurt him."

38
00:03:31.995 --> 00:03:38.511
"You won't." Cratchit's wife held up the bundle, beaming with joy.

39
00:03:38.512 --> 00:03:43.127
Cratchit moved closer to take the baby from his wife.

40
00:03:43.128 --> 00:03:48.337
As Cratchit gently rocked him, the baby's large eyes gazed up at him.

41
00:03:48.338 --> 00:03:53.026
"You're so beautiful," Cratchit whispered to the baby.

42
00:03:53.027 --> 00:03:59.398
"My perfect boy, my beloved little Tiny Tim."

43
00:03:59.399 --> 00:04:03.794
"Tiny Tim?" Scrooge shook his head.

44
00:04:03.795 --> 00:04:10.316
"I believe he's the youngest of the children whom Cratchit feeds on his small salary.

45
00:04:10.317 --> 00:04:15.369
But, spirit, what does this family scene have to do with me?"

46
00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:20.351
Instead of providing an answer, the spirit asked a question.

47
00:04:20.352 --> 00:04:25.433
"Who decides the amount of your clerk's salary?"

48
00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:29.103
"Well, I do, of course!" Scrooge snapped.

49
00:04:29.104 --> 00:04:34.949
"I pay him what his work is worth to me. No more, no less."

50
00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:38.554
The ghost tilted its head in reply.

51
00:04:38.555 --> 00:04:43.737
Scrooge frowned, reading disapproval in the ghost's expression.

52
00:04:43.738 --> 00:04:50.067
He glanced back at Bob Cratchit, who was still cooing over his newborn son.

53
00:04:50.068 --> 00:04:54.907
"I am a good man of business," Scrooge muttered.

54
00:04:54.908 --> 00:05:00.007
"You may ask anyone, spirit, and they'll tell you the same thing.

55
00:05:00.008 --> 00:05:07.315
Besides, if I paid my employee more than his worth, everyone would call me a sentimental fool.

56
00:05:07.316 --> 00:05:11.461
I would never receive fair treatment in business again.

57
00:05:11.462 --> 00:05:17.909
Instead my associates would try to cheat me and take advantage of my weakness."

58
00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.583
The Ghost of Christmas Past didn't say anything.

59
00:05:21.584 --> 00:05:28.840
Instead it touched Scrooge's heart again, transporting both of them into a modest sitting room.

60
00:05:28.841 --> 00:05:33.832
A man and a woman were sitting in front of a crackling fire.

61
00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:38.327
Scrooge went pale, instantly recognizing the woman.

62
00:05:38.328 --> 00:05:49.939
"No, spirit! I beg you with all my heart, do not show me this part of my past!"

